6444324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444324 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444324 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444324 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186444295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444324 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d4649305-d5dc-5854-97b6-ea90ae323c38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444325 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444325 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132071" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444325 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444325 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $r(t)=⟨t,\\,\\sqrt{t}⟩$ as a function of $t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444325 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "076128a8-7495-5bb6-b7b4-2c35cb671771" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444325 #edb/ref 1074 "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4t}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4t+1}{4t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4t+1}}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}} & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} &  \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}})}{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{4t+1}}{2\\sqrt{t}})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{(4t+1)^{3}}}{8\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{(4t+1)^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444326 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444326 #edb/ref 1069 "q-165246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444326 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444326 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨6t,\\,{t}^{3},\\,3{t}^{2}⟩,$ use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$ \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that* ${r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=18(-{t}^{2}\\,i-2\\,j+2t\\,k).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444326 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0b5962d7-05b0-532b-8c6d-f094e8fba0fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444326 #edb/ref 1074 "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,6t⟩ \\\\  & =3⟨2,\\,{t}^{2},\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =3\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{({t}^{2})}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{4+{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =3({t}^{2}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $({t}^{2}+2)>0$ for any real value $t.$\n\nSince we are told that\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=18(-{t}^{2}\\,i-2\\,j+2t\\,k),\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =18\\sqrt{{(-{t}^{2})}^{2}+{(-2)}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =18\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =18\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =18({t}^{2}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18({t}^{2}+2)}{{(3({t}^{2}+2))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3{({t}^{2}+2)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444327 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444327 #edb/ref 1069 "q-64755" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444327 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444327 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the curvature of the curve $r(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩$, as a function of $t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444327 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b9a8797-8d36-57ec-bbd4-59c107c7ab69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444327 #edb/ref 1074 "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩=⟨8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}}=\\sqrt{100{cos}^{2}⁡2t}=10|\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t),-6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩=⟨-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,0⟩\\times ⟨-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & 0 \\\\ -16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t & 12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & -6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ -16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t & 12\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =(96\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t-96\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{0}{(10|\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t|)^{3}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444328 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444328 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86796" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444328 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444328 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨6-3{t}^{2},\\,4,\\,6t-2⟩$, use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature as a function of $t.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444328 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ed8ed9c-cb01-554d-a0b3-14bce9a90d30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444328 #edb/ref 1074 "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6-3{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-2)⟩=⟨-6t,\\,0,\\,6⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-6t)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{t}^{2}+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36({t}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative ${r}^{″}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-6t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6)⟩=⟨-6,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨-6t,0,6⟩\\times ⟨-6,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -6t & 0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-6t & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-6t & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-36\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{36}{{(6\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1})}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{6({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444329 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444329 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131973" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444329 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444329 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{3}t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,{t}^{3}⟩$ for $t\\ge 0,$ use the cross product curvature formula to compute the curvature at the point $P(0,0,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444329 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "449eabe4-56aa-5632-8405-aab3edd3f407" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444329 #edb/ref 1074 "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$